---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="SPF Softfail vs Hardfail: ~all vs -all Explained"
  description="SPF softfail (~all) vs hardfail (-all): what each qualifier means, how receivers treat them, and how to choose based on your verified sender inventory and forwarding risk."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/email-authentication/" class="hover:text-accent">Email Authentication</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">SPF Softfail vs Hardfail</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">SPF Softfail vs Hardfail: <code class="font-mono">~all</code> vs <code class="font-mono">-all</code></h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
      The difference between <code class="font-mono text-accent-soft">~all</code> and <code class="font-mono text-accent-soft">-all</code> is the instruction you give receiving servers about unauthorized senders. This page explains what each qualifier actually means — and what it does not.
    </p>

    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2>Terminology note</h2>
      <p>
        The SPF result for <code class="font-mono">-all</code> is called <strong class="text-ink">fail</strong> (not "hardfail" — that term is common but is not the RFC-defined result name). The result for <code class="font-mono">~all</code> is <strong class="text-ink">softfail</strong>. This page uses both the common terms and the RFC 7208 <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7208#section-2.6" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft" rel="noopener">§2.6</a> result names.
      </p>

      <h2>Side-by-side comparison</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60">
        <table class="pdns-table">
          <thead><tr><th></th><th><code class="font-mono">~all</code> (softfail)</th><th><code class="font-mono">-all</code> (fail)</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">SPF result</td><td class="text-sm text-warn">softfail</td><td class="text-sm text-fail">fail</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">What it tells the receiver</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">"This sender is probably not authorized, but I'm not certain"</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">"This sender is not authorized"</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Receiver action</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Receiver decides — typically accept but may penalize in spam filtering</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Receiver decides — typically reject, but not guaranteed</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Risk of legitimate mail blocked</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Lower — unlisted senders still get through</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Higher — any unlisted sender will fail SPF</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Protection against spoofing</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Weak — spoofers may still reach the inbox (penalized but not blocked)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Stronger — spoofed mail gets an SPF fail result that receivers can act on</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>What neither qualifier guarantees</h2>
      <p>
        Receiving systems apply their own disposition policy. Neither qualifier <em>guarantees</em> a specific outcome:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5">
        <li><code class="font-mono">-all</code> does <strong class="text-ink">not</strong> guarantee rejection. A receiver may still accept the mail, quarantine it, or ignore the SPF result entirely depending on their filtering stack.</li>
        <li><code class="font-mono">~all</code> does <strong class="text-ink">not</strong> guarantee delivery. A receiver may still spam-folder the mail, especially if other signals (content, reputation, engagement) are negative.</li>
        <li>An SPF policy checker — including ours — <strong class="text-ink">cannot</strong> establish inbox placement. It can only report what your published policy says. Whether a specific message reaches the inbox depends on the receiving server's filtering decisions.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Is <code class="font-mono">-all</code> required for DMARC?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong class="text-ink">No.</strong> DMARC does not require <code class="font-mono">-all</code>. DMARC evaluates whether an <em>aligned</em> SPF pass or DKIM pass exists (see <a href="/dmarc-alignment/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DMARC alignment explained</a>). A message can pass DMARC with <code class="font-mono">~all</code> as long as the sender is listed and the domain aligns. Conversely, a message with <code class="font-mono">-all</code> that is not sent from a listed server will fail SPF — but if DKIM is aligned and passes, DMARC can still pass.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is a common misconception. Switching to <code class="font-mono">-all</code> does not improve DMARC alignment and is not a prerequisite for DMARC enforcement.
      </p>

      <h2>How to choose</h2>
      <p>
        The choice should be based on your <strong class="text-ink">verified sender inventory</strong> and <strong class="text-ink">operational risk</strong>, not on a mandatory progression from softfail to hardfail.
      </p>

      <h3>Use <code class="font-mono">~all</code> when:</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5">
        <li>You are not confident every legitimate sender is listed (new services, departments adding tools, third-party integrations).</li>
        <li>Your mail is frequently forwarded (forwarders break SPF; <code class="font-mono">~all</code> gives them a chance to deliver).</li>
        <li>You are still building your sender inventory and need a safety margin.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Use <code class="font-mono">-all</code> when:</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5">
        <li>You have verified every legitimate sender through DMARC reports over several weeks or months.</li>
        <li>All senders — including ESPs, CRMs, and billing systems — are listed and their DKIM signatures are aligned.</li>
        <li>You understand that any future unlisted sender will fail SPF and plan to add it before it starts sending.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>What "verified sender inventory" means</h3>
      <p>
        Before switching to <code class="font-mono">-all</code>, review your DMARC aggregate reports (or <code class="font-mono">Authentication-Results</code> headers from test messages) to identify every IP and domain that legitimately sends as your domain. Each should either be listed in your SPF or have an aligned DKIM signature. The <a href="/dmarc-report-analyzer/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DMARC Report Analyzer</a> can help parse these reports.
      </p>

      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5">
        <li><strong class="text-ink">Switching to <code class="font-mono">-all</code> before verifying senders:</strong> legitimate mail starts failing, potentially including password resets and order confirmations.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">Treating <code class="font-mono">-all</code> as a DMARC requirement:</strong> it is not. DMARC can pass with <code class="font-mono">~all</code>.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">Assuming <code class="font-mono">-all</code> blocks all spoofing:</strong> receivers decide what to do with an SPF fail result. Some may still deliver the mail.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">Forgetting forwarding:</strong> forwarded mail breaks SPF. If forwarders are part of your workflow, ensure DKIM is aligned so DMARC can pass independently of SPF.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Related</h2>
      <ul class="grid gap-2">
        <li><a href="/spf-record/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">SPF records: the complete guide</a></li>
        <li><a href="/dmarc-alignment/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DMARC alignment explained</a></li>
        <li><a href="/spf-record-checker/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">SPF Record Checker (free tool)</a></li>
        <li><a href="/dmarc/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DMARC: the complete guide</a></li>
      </ul>

      <p class="mt-6 text-xs text-ink-faint">
        Primary reference: <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7208#section-4.6.2" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft" rel="noopener">RFC 7208 §4.6.2 (Qualifiers)</a> and <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7208#section-2.6" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft" rel="noopener">§2.6 (Result Definitions)</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
